INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "3CX DesktopApp 2023 compromise evidence batch", "source_url": "SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-008; SRC-009", "text": "Public-source findings: Mandiant reports the initial compromise vector of 3CX's network was malicious software downloaded from the Trading Technologies website, specifically a tampered X_TRADER installer. The infected X_TRADER installation on a 3CX employee's computer enabled attackers to move through 3CX, harvest credentials, move laterally, compromise Windows and macOS build environments, and corrupt the 3CX installer/software distribution. Mandiant identified VEILEDSIGNAL and modules in the X_TRADER chain; in the 3CX chain, trojanized DesktopApp versions contained SUDDENICON, which retrieved C2 data from encrypted GitHub-hosted icons and downloaded ICONICSTEALER. On Windows build infrastructure, Mandiant reports TAXHAUL and COLDCAT with DLL search-order hijacking via IKEEXT and LocalSystem execution; on the macOS build server, POOLRAT persisted via Launch Daemons. Mandiant tracks the activity as UNC4736, a suspected North Korean nexus cluster, and assesses with moderate confidence that it is related to financially motivated North Korean AppleJeus activity. It cites overlap with POOLRAT previously reported in AppleJeus and Google TAG reporting on Trading Technologies. Kaspersky reports trojanized MSI installers, malicious DLL loading, GitHub icon C2 retrieval, an infostealer, and targeted deployment of the Gopuram backdoor to fewer than ten machines, especially cryptocurrency companies. Kaspersky attributes the campaign to Lazarus with medium-to-high confidence based on Gopuram coexisting with AppleJeus, cryptocurrency targeting, shared loader-shellcode patterns, and infrastructure/domain overlaps, while noting some shellcode was open source and could be used by others. WIRED summarizes Mandiant's chain as Trading Technologies X_TRADER -> 3CX employee PC -> lateral movement -> 3CX development server/build environment -> corrupted installer -> broad customer distribution, and reports alternate public names Kimsuky, Emerald Sleet, and Velvet Chollima, described as widely believed North Korea-linked. Singapore CSA confirms trojanized Windows and macOS DesktopApp versions and capabilities to harvest system information, browser data, and stored credentials."}
